# Cached tiles are drawn at start-up whatever the network is doing.
#
# The claim: the map's cache is served by a thread that never waits on the network, and the tiles
# the cache does not hold go to a pool of fetch threads - five, as GMap.NET's Core.cs:62 has it -
# so a tile that is not cached costs one fetch thread its request, not the screen its cached
# tiles. Before this, one thread did both, and a single uncached tile at the first view held every
# cached tile behind it for the fetch's whole timeout: a full cache took five to ten seconds to
# appear after start-up.
#
# The cache is laid out as the C# writes it (tests/gui/seed-csharp-cache.py), a 3x3 block around
# SITL's home at every zoom with the centre tile left out, so the view the map frames round the
# vehicle has cached tiles and one it must fetch. The fetch goes to tests/gui/silent-proxy.py,
# which accepts and never answers; nothing leaves the machine, and the fetch hangs for its ten
# second timeout. The cached tiles must be on screen regardless, from the disk, with nothing
# fetched. The view at this window size holds six tiles, five cached and the hole;
# `map.tiles.disk >= 5` is the number, because the one-thread store took the requests newest
# first and served two or three before it reached the hole and hung.
#
# Needs SITL copter on tcp:127.0.0.1:5760: the map frames the vehicle, and with nothing to frame
# it draws a graticule and asks for no tiles. Run as
#
#   tools/gui-test.sh tests/gui/tiles-startup.gui -- tcp:127.0.0.1:5760

tiles on
env MP_TILE_CACHE $WORK/gmapcache
env MP_TILE_SOURCE osm
env HTTPS_PROXY http://127.0.0.1:38519
env HTTP_PROXY http://127.0.0.1:38519
setup python3 tests/gui/seed-csharp-cache.py --block 3 --hole $WORK/gmapcache
setup python3 tests/gui/silent-proxy.py --port 38519 --work $WORK

screen fly
window 1600x1200
settle 4

expect map.source osm
expect map.tiles.fetched 0
expect map.tiles.disk >= 5
expect map.tiles.drawn >= 5
expect map.tiles.missing >= 1
